  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "At TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, Flock Safety CEO Garrett Langley defended the company amid growing scrutiny of its license plate readers and lesser-known Raven audio-detection system, while activists protested outside. The article discusses misuse incidents in San Francisco, Mountain View, El Cerrito, and San Jose; Antioch's switch from Flock to Axon; and Flock's planned new safeguards including a 7-day retention policy taking effect in January.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Under state law, it's illegal for federal agencies or out-of-state police to access the network. However, audits and lawsuits have revealed instances of it happening anyway, in cities like San Francisco, Mountain View and El Cerrito.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    },
    {
      "quote": "With Flock we opt into a network of sharing at the state level. At Axon, if anybody wants to share data with us, they have to make a specific request of us and it has to be reciprocated by us.",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock says it is working on new safeguards, including a policy set to take effect in January under which data will automatically be deleted after seven days, unless detectives preserve it for an active criminal investigation.",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    }
  ],
